---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base title="ASCII FX — guide" active="guide">
  <h1>Guide</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    One mental model everywhere: a <b>profile</b> (compiled font data) plus a <b>source</b> go into an exact
    matcher; the resulting glyph field is composited in one draw; interactions live downstream of matching.
  </p>

  <h2>React</h2>
  <pre><code>import &#123; AsciiImage, AsciiVideo, AsciiCanvas &#125; from '@ascii-fx/react'

&lt;AsciiImage src="/hero.jpg" alt="Portrait" profile=&#123;profileRef&#125; columns=&#123;160&#125; color="full"
  interaction=&#123;&#123; type: 'reveal', radius: 0.18, feather: 0.08 &#125;&#125; /&gt;

&lt;AsciiVideo src="/clip.mp4" profile=&#123;profileRef&#125; /&gt;
&lt;AsciiCanvas source=&#123;myCanvas&#125; renderMode="continuous" profile=&#123;profileRef&#125; /&gt;</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>SSR renders the accessible original media in a layout-stable wrapper; the client swaps in the canvas with no flash or shift. Files ship <code>'use client'</code> for Next App Router.</li>
    <li><code>alt</code> is required (<code>alt=""</code> for decorative). The fallback stays in the accessibility tree; the canvas is <code>aria-hidden</code>.</li>
    <li><code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> disables interactions, keeps the static ASCII.</li>
    <li>Prop changes map to <code>setOptions</code> — the renderer is only recreated when profile/backend change. Ref handle: <code>&#123; renderer, render, capture, getSupport &#125;</code>.</li>
    <li>Omit <code>profile</code> and a runtime <code>monospace</code> profile is generated with Canvas — good for trying things; precompile for production.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Vanilla</h2>
  <pre><code>const ascii = await createAsciiRenderer(&#123; canvas, profile, backend: 'auto' &#125;)
ascii.setSource(imageOrVideoOrCanvas)
ascii.render()        // one-shot
ascii.start()         // continuous: rVFC for video, rAF otherwise
ascii.setOptions(&#123; columns: 200, color: 'mono' &#125;)
ascii.setInteraction(&#123; type: 'push' &#125;)
ascii.pointer.set(x01, y01)
const frame = await ascii.captureFrame()   // explicit readback only
ascii.destroy()</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>backend: 'auto'</code>: WebGPU when a device is acquirable, otherwise the exact CPU matcher + Canvas2D composite. Identical output; check <code>ascii.backend</code> or <code>getAsciiSupport()</code>.</li>
    <li>Video work: <code>temporal: true</code> skips unchanged cells exactly; <code>ascii.invalidate(rect)</code> rematches only a region; <code>adaptiveResolution: true</code> trades columns for frame rate with hysteresis.</li>
    <li>Text output: <code>frame.toText() / toAnsi() / toHTML()</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three.js (WebGPURenderer)</h2>
  <pre><code>import * as THREE from 'three/webgpu'
import &#123; AsciiPass &#125; from '@ascii-fx/three'

const renderer = new THREE.WebGPURenderer(&#123; canvas &#125;)
await renderer.init()
const pass = new AsciiPass(&#123; profile, renderer, columns: 160, color: 'full' &#125;)
await pass.init()
// per frame, instead of renderer.render():
pass.render(scene, camera)</code></pre>
  <p>
    Matching runs on Three's own GPUDevice — zero readback. WebGL renderers are unsupported by design: exact
    matching there would require per-frame GPU→CPU copies. Render targets are linear-light, so the pass
    sRGB-encodes before matching.
  </p>

  <h2>React Three Fiber</h2>
  <pre><code>&lt;Canvas gl=&#123;async (props) =&gt; &#123; const r = new THREE.WebGPURenderer(props); await r.init(); return r &#125;&#125;&gt;
  &lt;mesh&gt;…&lt;/mesh&gt;
  &lt;AsciiEffect profile=&#123;profileRef&#125; columns=&#123;180&#125; interaction="reveal" /&gt;
&lt;/Canvas&gt;</code></pre>

  <h2>Build-time compilation</h2>
  <pre><code>// ascii-fx.config.ts
import &#123; defineAsciiConfig &#125; from '@ascii-fx/vite'
export default defineAsciiConfig(&#123;
  profiles: &#123; default: &#123; font: './fonts/GeistMono-Regular.woff2', charset: 'ascii' &#125; &#125;,
  frames:   &#123; hero: &#123; image: './src/hero.png', columns: 140, color: 'full' &#125; &#125;,
&#125;)

import profileRef from 'virtual:ascii-profile/default'
import heroRef from 'virtual:ascii-frame/hero'
const frame = await loadFrame(heroRef, await loadProfile(profileRef)) // zero matching at runtime</code></pre>
  <p>
    Profiles compile once and cache content-addressed; fonts hot-reload in dev. Static frames precompute the
    matching itself — ideal for hero art on SSR-heavy pages. The CLI (<code>ascii-fx profile build / frame build /
    inspect</code>) does the same without Vite.
  </p>

  <h2>Restricting the character set</h2>
  <pre><code>// at build time: compile exactly the charset you want
profiles: &#123; rain: &#123; font: './fonts/GeistMono-Regular.woff2', characters: '01 ' &#125; &#125;

// at runtime: narrow any built profile without recompiling
import &#123; loadProfile, subsetProfile &#125; from '@ascii-fx/core'
const profile = subsetProfile(await loadProfile(profileRef), '01 ')</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>subsetProfile</code> carries each glyph's raster data over exactly, so matching a subset is identical
    to matching a profile compiled with that charset. Unknown or duplicate characters are errors. Charsets can
    hold any code point the font has outlines for — <code>ascii-blocks</code> (▀ ▄ ▚ ▓) ships built in, and
    braille (⠏ ⣠ ⣿) works wherever the font provides it. Color emoji do not fit this pipeline: bitmap fonts
    have no outlines to rasterize, double-width advances break the monospace check, and per-cell color fitting
    would repaint them regardless.
  </p>
</Base>
